= Field notes on making things
_A short manuscript · original demonstration text_

== Chapter one: Begin

Every project begins with a question. Sometimes it is small enough to write on a single line. Sometimes it takes a few pages to discover what we meant to ask.

=== Keep a notebook

Write the first observation before it becomes an explanation. A notebook gives the work a memory, and the next page a starting point.

=== Find the question

Return to your notes. Look for the detail that appears more than once. What seems ordinary may be the center of the whole piece.

== Chapter two: Connect

Give each idea a place. Headings make a long document easier to navigate while the argument is still taking shape.

=== Choose a sequence

Move from the example to the explanation. Let each section answer one question and make the next question possible.

=== Follow the outline

An outline shows what the draft is doing. It also shows what is missing. Use it to move through the manuscript and return to the part that needs attention.

#pagebreak()
== Chapter three: Revise

Read for the reader. Keep the detail that helps them understand; remove the sentence that only explains why you wrote it.

=== Compare versions

An earlier draft can remind us where an idea began. Revision is easier when we can return to the path we took.

=== Share the work

A clear page lets the writing speak. Finish the thought, review the pages, and make something that another person can read.
